Vita: --vita-frame-overlap (platform/vita/aurora/frame_overlap.hpp). With the GX worker (0007),
aurora_end_frame may publish the frame and return without waiting; the worker goes on processing
it while the game thread runs the next game tick, and the frame is settled (waited for, its bytes
dropped, presented) at the game's next GXDrawDone (TP's, at the end of the tick's draw phase), or
at the latest before a second tick or the presentation draw. Commands written in between
belong to the next frame: they are not published (publish() stays off between frames) and keep
their stream positions when settle_frame moves them to the front of the buffer.

GXSetDrawDone records its position, so the frame end still waits for the draw-done callback, as
early in the frame as the game put it (the callback then runs before end returns, as with the
drain). drain() first lets the platform settle a pending frame (GXDrawDone between frames), and
AuroraSetViewportPolicy tells the platform that the next update changes worker-read state. The
game thread's time blocked on the worker (drain and the new waits) is counted for the receipts.
For --vita-frame-overlap=verify the command processor reports the game memory it reads in place
(indexed matrix rows, array bases, static display lists) to a weak platform hook.
Off (the default), nothing changes: the platform still drains at the frame end.

